You're pretty sure of something that doesn't happen
to be true. Back in those days, if you wanted to
find out the warnings about your code, you didn't
trust the compiler, you ran the "lint" program
separately over your code. Undeclared variables
defaulted to "int", and it was a constant source of
bugs that the did so.

Certainly the C compilers I used when I started
using C in 1983 on IBM mainframes did not report
undeclared variables, nor did the BSD 4.2 or 4.3
Unix C compilers I used on VAXen from 1985 to 1988,
at least at the beginning of that span.

Somewhere after that, though, along with prototypes,
came compiler reports of undeclared variables,
because now with a prototype, you _always_ had a way
to declare a variable before using it, even if the
"variable" was the value type of a function to be
first declared further down in the code.

